# 12. TRANSFER PIPELINE

Design a streaming pipeline.

Conceptual sequence:

File Source
    ↓
File Reader
    ↓
Chunk Manager
    ↓
Bounded Queue
    ↓
Transport Scheduler
    ↓
Encrypted Transport
    ↓
Receiver Queue
    ↓
Integrity Verification
    ↓
File Writer
    ↓
Atomic Finalization

The architecture must be asynchronous and cancellation-aware.

## 12.1 Chunking

Do not use one fixed chunk size for every environment.

Start with configurable chunk sizes.

Initial experimental values:
- 256 KiB.
- 1 MiB.
- 4 MiB.

Benchmark them.

Use receiver feedback, network characteristics and memory pressure to select an appropriate configuration.

Do not claim that a particular chunk size is universally optimal.

## 12.2 Backpressure

Use bounded channels.

The receiver must be able to signal when it cannot accept more data.

The sender must respond appropriately.

Avoid:
- Unbounded queues.
- Unlimited buffering.
- Excessive task creation.
- Repeated unnecessary memory allocation.
- Blocking the async runtime.

## 12.3 File I/O

Use efficient asynchronous or carefully managed file I/O.

Avoid loading entire files into RAM.

Use bounded memory.

Use temporary destination files.

Verify file integrity before finalizing.

Never overwrite an existing file without an explicit and understandable user decision.

## 12.4 Parallelism

Implement configurable concurrency.

Benchmark:
- Sequential file transfer.
- Multiple file transfers.
- Multiple streams.
- Parallel reading.
- Parallel hashing.

Do not parallelize blindly.

The engine should avoid oversubscribing CPU, storage and network resources.

